  "body": "Greetings,\r\n\r\nI ran into on this on my machine using LetsEncrypt certificates, whose default configuration lives at `/etc/letsencrypt/live/(domain name)/fullchain.pem`. These files have mode o+r, preventing anyone other than root from writing to them.\r\n\r\nThe error I got on startup was:\r\n\r\n```\r\n File \"/usr/lib/python2.7/dist-packages/synapse/config/tls.py\", line 130, in generate_files\r\n   with open(tls_private_key_path, \"w\") as private_key_file:\r\n  IOError: [Errno 13] Permission denied: '/etc/letsencrypt/live/isengard.tkware.us/privkey.pem'\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt seems very strange that Synapse would be opening keyfiles with write mode especially considering that `open('file', 'w')` truncates the file if it already exists! Given that this keyfile is shared with other TLS-using applications on the system, this could have been painful for me if it had actually worked.\r\n\r\nIn any case, I copied the keyfile aside and gave the copy o+w permissions, and there were no further problems.\r\n\r\nJust wondering if this is intentional :)",
